{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was a member of the House of Toulouse and had become Count of Tripoli through his marriage to Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III's early life is not well documented but it is known that he was raised in the court of his father in Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his death in the Battle of Inab. At the time Raymond III was only around 12 years old and the regency was held by his mother Hodierna. However Raymond III soon took control of the county and began to expand its borders through a series of military campaigns.\n\n*The Battle of Harenc*\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi during the Battle of Harenc. He was imprisoned in Aleppo for several years until he was released in 1173. During his captivity the county of Tripoli was governed by his cousin Amalric I of Jerusalem.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nAfter his release from captivity Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed his rule. He continued to expand the county's borders and strengthened its defenses. Raymond III also played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a close ally of King Amalric I. He died in 1187 and was succeeded by his cousin Raymond IV Count of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by a series of military campaigns and power struggles with neighboring states. Despite being captured and imprisoned for several years he was able to maintain control of the county of Tripoli and expand its borders. His legacy as a military leader and statesman has endured and he remains an important figure in the history of the Crusader states.", "logp